## 3. Hybrid Approach (Y.js)

### Core Algorithm & Conflict Resolution

Y.js combines CRDT principles with optimizations that address pure CRDT limitations.

**Key innovations:**

```javascript
// 1. Block-based structure instead of character-based
class YBlock {
  constructor(id, content) {
    this.id = id;              // Unique block ID
    this.content = content;    // Array of characters (not individual IDs)
    this.left = null;          // Left neighbor block
    this.right = null;         // Right neighbor block
  }
}

// Instead of:
// [H][e][l][l][o] (5 CRDT items)

// Y.js uses:
// [Hello] (1 block containing 5 characters)

// Reduces metadata overhead by ~10x
```

**Conflict resolution:**
```javascript
// Combines CRDT convergence with practical optimizations
class YText {
  // When two users type in sequence at same position
  insert(position, text) {
    // Try to extend existing block (common case)
    const block = this.findBlockAt(position);

    if (block.canExtend(position, this.clientId)) {
      // Fast path: append to existing block
      block.content += text;
      return { type: 'extend', blockId: block.id, text };
    } else {
      // Create new block (concurrent edit)
      const newBlock = new YBlock(this.generateId(), text);
      this.insertBlock(newBlock, position);
      return { type: 'insert', block: newBlock };
    }
  }
}
```

